over the past generation brands have  shifted from simply static images to  dynamic system this three-part series  entitled the 22nd century brand is  brought to you by design Singapore the  national agency for design enabling  Singapore to use design for economic  growth and to improve lives in these  discussions we will be looking at the  origin of Brad's how brands function in  our contemporary cultural landscape and  the future of brands where it's all  headed my name is hunter Tara I'm the  founder and CEO of an international  brand design consultancy called  syndicate acts over the past 25 years  I've had the opportunity to work with  some of the world's leading  organizations across culture commerce  and technology and I've helped them  develop clear brand stories and I've  helped them articulate their value  proposition I've worked with  multinational corporations such as  Netflix Nike GE Unilever Lululemon OD  and Asics - some of the world's leading  museums and cultural institutions  including the Victoria and Albert Museum  and the John F Kennedy Center for the  Performing Arts through these  collaborative partnerships I've had the  opportunity to see how brands really  work and to witness the power they have  in our collective consciousness this  series is intended to share some of the  insights and secrets I've been able to  gather over the course of my career in  our last discussion we looked at the  origin of brass  tracing their cultural meaning from  ancient Rome through the advent of the  21st century in this installment we're  going to examine the role of brands in  our contemporary cultural landscape and  our collective consciousness and examine  the dynamics shift from static brand  identities to a new set of phenomena  whereby brands themselves have now  become the focal point for interaction  and transaction we're also going to  explore how the role of the designer is  changing in this contemporary context  especially with the rise of  sophisticated technologies data and  artificial intelligence over the past  generation brands have shifted from  simply static images to dynamic systems  where every part of an organizational  image and reputation is designed the  shift is due in part to the ability of  designers to achieve levels of visual  sophistication through digital  technology that was previously not  possible but it also corresponds with a  fundamental change in global culture and  commerce as globalized organizations  became more intricate they increasingly  relied on new design techniques to  manage this complexity brands more than  ever rely on a complex set of  partnerships associations and  affiliations which means they need a new  set of devices and a new set of tools  both visually as well as in terms of the  nomenclature and language that they use  to remain clear an increasingly crowded  and complex marketplace for example if  we consider the case of GE under the  leadership of CEO Jack Welch during the  1990s a company that had previously  produced household electronics  lightbulbs toasters  now owned a television network an  investment bank a jet engine division  medical imaging devices and wind  turbines who's an incredibly broad and  varied corporate entity but to make  sense of a company like GE as a single  organization designers now needed a new  set of flexible approaches to present a  coherent image of the company one that  would be recognizable for both end-users  and the investment community this has  led to a whole new Lexus con in the  world of brands and a virtual science of  branding itself companies and  organizations now need to express their  mission vision and values and establish  their visual hierarchy through brand  architecture and messaging guidelines  with the rise of the new economy in the  1990s and through the runners of at  based businesses which came to the  forefront with the global prevalence of  the iPhone first released in 2007 the  nature of branding and brands has  fundamentally shifted once again whereas  older brands were primarily an image  which stood for a set of principles  today the brand itself has become a  platform for participation and exchange  [Music]  first of all what does it mean for brand  to be participatory for me this is the  first and most important distinction  from the traditional logic of branding  now brands themselves facilitate and  encourage action in the case of Airbnb  is to belong anywhere for tik-tok is to  inspire creativity and bring joy for  Twitter it's to give everyone everywhere  the power to create and share ideas  for Facebook it's to give people the  power to build community and bring the  world closer together  [Music]  for LinkedIn to connect the world's  professionals to make them more  productive and successful and of course  for Google to organize the world's  information and make it universally  accessible useful  each of these brands are built to be  participatory in some way there is a  clear call to action and an invitation  to participate the more people who  participate the richer the brand  experience and the more robust the  platform we each have our own profiles  and we interact with friends and  strangers alike through their digital  platforms the second part of the  contemporary brand is exchange  what does exchange mean in this context  well all brands have always been  transactional in other words goods and  services have always been bought and  sold today brands encourage exchange on  a higher order everything from social  networking to the sharing of content all  the way to buying and selling and even  trading and bartering more archaic forms  of transaction have become modern again  well there is undeniably a sophisticated  technology at work it is in the space of  the brand where all of the action  actually happens and through Facebook's  mission the technology encourages me to  connect with a broad series of friends  and friends of friends all within the  safe space of the Facebook Brad  Uber's mission is to bring  transportation for everyone everywhere  in other words their mission is to  connect people indeed today every like  every food order every purchase every  friend request  we birthday photo every mean post exists  within the confines of a brand and the  more of us who participate in exchange  the richer that brand experience becomes  this has given brands and even deeper  role in our everyday lives and in  certain cases has displaced more  traditional forms of human interaction  it has given brands an incredible power  over our consciousness and in many cases  uber being a fantastic example of how  the boundaries have blurred in terms of  how we engage brands a single person can  now be an employee a customer and an  ambassador for a brand and even more  strangely fulfill the same roles for a  competitive brand simultaneously think  of an uber driver who also works for  lyft and Juno at the same time this is  both an incredibly exciting and an  incredibly scary time for brad's and the  question is how do they remain relevant  and mission driven in this increasingly  disruptive landscape and what does it  mean for the designer whose role has  shifted from the 20th century ideal of  the author to today that of an editor or  a curator or even an impresario  first off there has never been a better  time to be a designer global brands  certainly led by the example of Apple  have given the broader public a deeper  understanding how great design can  impact our lives and how design can make  a positive impact in the world moreover  to be a designer today means not just  working on architecture or visual  identities or fashion or websites  increasingly it means working across  these media simultaneously to ensure the  consistency of the brand design across  all public facing touch points the idea  of the designer today is more expansive  than at any point in history all of that  said how designers work and even if they  work is changing first the rise of  artificial intelligence has meant that  there are now online platforms think of  the Wix logo maker that can generate a  professional-looking brand by answering  just a few questions and clicks of the  mouse  next globalization and the ability to  seamlessly collaborate through online  platforms has meant that companies and  organizations can work with any designer  anywhere including parts of the world  that are far less expensive than the  traditional design at the Centers of New  York London Amsterdam Milan and Tokyo in  the Kovan 19 world I'm working with  project teams in Singapore Madrid London  New York and Dusseldorf on a daily basis  without ever having to leave my home and  lastly the rise of design thinking over  the past decade has meant that large  corporations have now internalized  design functions within their  organizations twenty-five years ago it  was quite rare to have a design  counterpart a client-side today it's  even more rare for a company to not have  someone with a design background  managing their brand communications  advertising and media but for all of the  societal changes that present challenges  for designers there are also the  corresponding set of new opportunities  today designers have to understand  global systems information technology  the global flow of capital and local  business customs in addition to a clear  understanding of form color composition  and hierarchy and of course designers  today can be their own entrepreneurs  controlling the means of production in  certain cases we don't need clients at  all paradoxically today the designer is  more integral and simultaneously more  disposable than ever before  and plays a larger role in the creation  development maintenance and management  of brands and what does the current  global pandemic mean for brands today  and moving forward and how do we as  designers position ourselves to gain the  necessary skills and anticipate the  brand challenges of tomorrow the next  installment called the future of brands  will give us a clue as to how brands  will be different in the future what  that means for us as designers  [Music]
 

